1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,713
At home, a toddler might be measured standing against a wall.

2
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:10,778
But WHO measures children under two lying down. Qiyas checks the difference.

3
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:18,160
A synthetic example: a boy, 607 days old, 78 centimetres, measured standing.

4
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:25,732
Measured as entered, length for age reads z minus 2.19: below the usual range.

5
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:32,917
WHO adds 0.7 centimetres for standing under two. Now it reads minus 1.94, in the usual range.

6
00:00:34,050 --> 00:00:36,425
Same child. Same tape measure.

7
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:41,485
Switch to English. Every number stays exactly the same.

8
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:51,472
Now a harder case. The weight entered is implausible, so Qiyas shows no number.

9
00:00:52,050 --> 00:00:59,350
But the parent answered yes: swelling of both feet. That is a warning sign, whatever the numbers say.

10
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:05,846
So the first thing on screen is: see a health worker today. No diagnosis.

11
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:10,949
A typical child: no alarm. Keep up regular check-ups.

12
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:21,148
One optional AI feature answers a fixed question from the approved text. It sits below the result and is not wired to it.

13
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:28,324
The data is WHO's own expanded tables, downloaded from WHO, values unmodified.

14
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:40,336
The tests feed WHO's own table rows back through the engine and expect minus three to plus three. 134 tests pass.

15
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:47,120
Bands and the urgent action come from WHO cut-offs in plain code. No AI in the maths.

16
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:54,358
Shipped: three WHO indicators, ages zero to five, Arabic and English.

17
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:02,565
Not shipped: BMI, school-age children, tracking over visits, a native app.

18
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:11,386
WHO did the math. Parents need the meaning. Credit to WHO Anthro and UNICEF's Bebbo, who came first.

19
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:19,511
Built by Dr. Ahmed Zayed, a physician and engineer. A prototype, not a diagnosis. Try it at the link below.
